  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nShowers and storms return to the area late tonight and on Thursday, \nas a cold frontal boundary sags south into the area, and a wave of \nlow pressure rides along the front into the region. Still quite a \nbit of uncertainty in exact details, but with anomalously high PW \nair, combined with enhanced ascent from the passing low, along with \noverall mean flow aligning with the frontal boundary potentially \nallowing for repeated convection to form along it, an axis of \nheavier rainfall remains possible across the Ohio Valley region in \nparticular, and this, combined with the already saturated \nconditions, has warranted issuance of a watch for flash flooding. \nPrecipitation will linger into Thursday when the wave is finally \nable to exit to our east and we dry out later in the day. An \nisolated strong to severe storm is also possible, with a damaging \nwind threat, although with any storm/wind gust, trees will fall in \nthe saturated conditions. \n\nAnother shortwave wave will pass across the south on Friday, \nproviding additional storms. This will be followed by additional \nshortwaves over the weekend into early next week as a deeper trough \ngets carved out across the area as low pressure digs south into the \nGreat Lakes region.",
